From e22133bb76980bef5509e255b401214598ec5d80 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alp Lab AB <contact@alplab.ai>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 16:10:19 +0200
Subject: i2c: rzg2l_riic: don't split a register read into two bus
 transactions

riic_xfer() ran every struct i2c_msg in a multi-message transfer as its
own independent START..STOP bus transaction.  dm_i2c_read() always
builds a register read as exactly two messages: msg[0] is a plain
write carrying only the register offset, msg[1] is the paired read.
riic_xfer() therefore ran msg[0] through riic_write_common() as a full
transaction (START, ADDR+W, offset byte, TEND wait, STOP) and then ran
msg[1] through riic_read_common(), which independently performs the
WHOLE combined operation itself (START, ADDR+W, offset byte again from
priv->offset, TEND wait, repeated START, ADDR+R, data, STOP).  Net
result: every register read sent the offset byte twice, as two
separate transactions with a spurious STOP between them, instead of
the single repeated-START transaction dm_i2c_read() intends.

On the ALP E1M-V2M DEEPX-rail bring-up patch's DA9292 PMIC accesses
(RIIC8 / BRD_I2C, board/renesas/rzv2n-dev/rzv2n-dev.c
alp_deepx_rail_bringup()) this desyncs the device badly enough that
the very first cold-boot register read (PMC_DEV_ID) times out
(riic_wait_for_icsr2() -> -ETIMEDOUT), and once this RIIC instance's
ICCR2 BBSY flag is left stuck set by an incompletely-recovered
transaction, every later transaction fails immediately with -EBUSY.
riic_ops only implements .xfer/.probe_chip (no bus-recovery op), so
"i2c reset" cannot clear the wedge either -- only a power cycle does,
by resetting the DA9292's own I2C engine.  Writes are unaffected:
dm_i2c_write() always builds a single combined message (offset + data
in one buffer), so riic_write_common() alone already does the right
single transaction for every DA9292 write in the rail sequence.

Recognise the standard write-offset-then-read message pair and skip
the redundant first transaction: stash the offset directly and let
riic_read_common() run the one genuine combined transaction.  Any
other message pattern (a lone write, or a bare read with no offset
message) falls through to the original per-message loop unchanged.

Restricted to a ONE-BYTE offset (msg[0].len == 1): riic_read_common()
itself only ever resends a single byte from priv->offset before the
restart (alen is hardcoded to 1, matching the pre-existing code's own
limitation there), so fast-pathing a genuinely multi-byte offset would
silently drop its trailing byte(s).  The on-module 24C128 EEPROM on
RIIC0 addresses with 2 bytes and is the only other dm_i2c_read() user
board_late_init() has -- it is deliberately left on the original,
unmodified per-message loop, so this change carries no behaviour
change for it either way.

This is a generic RIIC(rzg2l)/R9A09G056 driver defect, not specific to
RIIC8: every ONE-BYTE-offset register read on any of the SoC's nine
RIIC instances goes through this same riic_xfer() path.

Signed-off-by: Alp Lab AB <contact@alplab.ai>
---
 drivers/i2c/rzg2l_riic.c | 38 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 38 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/i2c/rzg2l_riic.c b/drivers/i2c/rzg2l_riic.c
index d84f1419..13e85635 100644
--- a/drivers/i2c/rzg2l_riic.c
+++ b/drivers/i2c/rzg2l_riic.c
@@ -598,8 +598,46 @@ force_exit:
 
 static int riic_xfer(struct udevice *dev, struct i2c_msg *msg, int nmsgs)
 {
+	struct riic_priv *priv = dev_get_priv(dev);
 	int ret;
 
+	/*
+	 * ALP: dm_i2c_read() always issues a combined transfer for a
+	 * register read -- msg[0] is a plain write carrying only the
+	 * register offset, msg[1] is the paired read.  riic_read_common()
+	 * below already performs that ENTIRE combined operation itself
+	 * (write the offset, repeated START, read) using priv->offset, so
+	 * running msg[0] through riic_write_common() first (as the generic
+	 * loop below does) sends the offset byte TWICE, as two independent
+	 * START..STOP bus transactions with a spurious STOP in between --
+	 * not the single repeated-START transaction dm_i2c_read() intends.
+	 *
+	 * On the DA9292 PMIC on RIIC8/BRD_I2C (E1M-V2M DEEPX rail, see the
+	 * DEEPX-rail-bringup patch) this desyncs the device's I2C engine
+	 * badly enough that the read either times out (-ETIMEDOUT) or
+	 * leaves this RIIC instance's ICCR2 BBSY flag stuck set, wedging
+	 * the bus for every later transaction (-EBUSY) until power-cycled
+	 * -- this driver implements no bus-recovery op, so "i2c reset"
+	 * cannot clear it either ("Not supported by the driver").
+	 *
+	 * Recognise the write-offset+read pattern and skip the redundant
+	 * first transaction: just stash the offset and let
+	 * riic_read_common() run the one genuine transaction.  Restricted to
+	 * a ONE-BYTE offset (msg[0].len == 1): riic_read_common() itself only
+	 * ever resends a single byte from priv->offset before the restart
+	 * (alen is hardcoded to 1 below, matching the original code's own
+	 * limitation), so a genuinely multi-byte offset -- e.g. the 24C128
+	 * EEPROM on RIIC0, which addresses with 2 bytes -- would lose its
+	 * second byte if fast-pathed here. Leave that case, and any other
+	 * message pattern (plain single write, e.g. dm_i2c_write(), or a
+	 * bare read), on the original per-message loop unchanged.
+	 */
+	if (nmsgs == 2 && !(msg[0].flags & I2C_M_RD) &&
+	    (msg[1].flags & I2C_M_RD) && msg[0].len == 1) {
+		priv->offset = msg[0].buf[0];
+		return riic_read_common(dev, &msg[1], 0, 1);
+	}
+
 	for (; nmsgs > 0; nmsgs--, msg++) {
 
 		if (msg->flags & I2C_M_RD)
